CAT = "llqpLifeProducts"; SEG = "LPR"
Q = [
('A couple wants coverage only while their 20-year mortgage is outstanding, at the lowest possible premium. Which policy fits best?',
 'A 20-year term policy', ['A term to 100 policy', 'A participating whole life policy', 'A universal life policy'],
 'Term insurance covers a set period at the lowest cost per dollar of coverage. Term to 100 and permanent policies charge for lifetime protection the couple does not need.'),
('A renewable term policy renews at the end of each term without new evidence of insurability. What happens to the premium at renewal?',
 'It rises to the new age rate', ['It stays at the original rate', 'It falls as cash value builds', 'It is set by fresh underwriting'],
 "Renewal is guaranteed, but the premium jumps to the guaranteed renewal rate for the insured's attained age. No medical evidence is asked for, so underwriting does not reset it."),
('The conversion privilege in a term policy lets the owner:',
 'Switch to permanent cover, no medical', ['Cash in the term cover early for value', 'Add a spouse at the original rate', 'Cut the premium after a new medical'],
 'Conversion exchanges the term policy for a permanent one, up to the converted amount, with no proof of insurability, usually before a stated age or date.'),
('Term to 100 coverage is best described as:',
 'Level premiums, little or no cash value', ['Rising premiums and a large cash value', 'A 100-year term that then renews again', 'Coverage that pays out only on reaching 100'],
 'Term to 100 has level premiums to age 100 (often paid up then) and generally no cash surrender value, which keeps it cheaper than whole life.'),
('A participating whole life policy pays dividends. These dividends are:',
 'Not guaranteed; based on results', ['Guaranteed in the contract each year', 'Paid only when the insured dies', 'Fixed at a rate tied to the prime rate'],
 "Policy dividends come from the participating account's experience (investment returns, mortality and expenses) and are declared each year by the insurer, so they can change."),
('A par whole life owner wants dividends to raise the death benefit with no medical evidence. Which dividend option does that?',
 'Paid-up additions', ['Cash payment', 'Premium reduction', 'Accumulation on deposit'],
 'Paid-up additions use each dividend as a single premium for a small block of fully paid permanent insurance, increasing the death benefit and cash value without underwriting.'),
('Compared with a yearly renewable term charge, a level cost of insurance in a universal life policy:',
 'Costs more early, less later', ['Costs less in every policy year', "Rises every year with the insured's age", 'Is charged only after age 65'],
 'Level COI is priced to stay flat for life, so it costs more than YRT in early years and less in later years. YRT rises with age each year.'),
("A universal life owner has the level death benefit option. As the account value grows, the insurer's net amount at risk:",
 'Falls', ['Rises', 'Stays the same', 'Doubles'],
 'With the level option the face amount includes the account value, so net amount at risk (face minus fund) shrinks as the fund grows, lowering the cost of insurance. The level plus account value option keeps it constant.'),
("Why does a universal life policy's account value grow free of annual tax?",
 'The policy is an exempt policy', ['It is held in an RRSP', 'Its fund is a seg fund', 'Its owner pays the tax in advance'],
 'A policy that meets the exempt test under the Income Tax Act accumulates value tax-sheltered. Overfunding past the exempt limit risks losing that treatment.'),
('Joint last-to-die coverage is often bought by a couple to:',
 'Pay tax due at the second death', ['Replace income at the first death', 'Cover the mortgage at either death', "Fund a child's education early"],
 'A joint last-to-die policy pays when the second spouse dies, when the deemed disposition of assets such as a cottage triggers capital gains tax. It is cheaper than two single-life policies.'),
('A joint first-to-die policy pays the death benefit:',
 'At the first death, then ends', ['At each death in turn', 'Only after both have died', 'At the first death, then renews'],
 'First-to-die pays once, on the first death of the insured lives, and the coverage ends. Some contracts let the survivor buy new coverage without evidence.'),
('A waiver of premium rider on a life policy:',
 'Waives premiums during disability', ['Pays a monthly disability income', 'Doubles the death benefit on claim', 'Refunds premiums paid at maturity'],
 'Waiver of premium keeps the policy in force by waiving premiums while the insured is totally disabled as defined, after a waiting period. It pays no income to the insured.'),
('An accidental death benefit rider pays an extra amount when the insured:',
 'Dies from an accident as defined', ['Dies from any cause in two years', 'Becomes disabled by an accident', 'Is diagnosed with a terminal illness'],
 "The rider adds a benefit only for death caused by an accident within the rider's terms, often within a set time of the injury. Disability is covered by other riders."),
('A guaranteed insurability rider lets the owner:',
 'Add coverage later with no medical', ['Renew the policy after age 85', 'Reinstate a lapsed policy anytime', 'Lower premiums after quitting smoking'],
 'The rider gives option dates (and often events such as marriage or a birth) to add coverage without medical evidence, up to set amounts and ages.'),
("A child term rider on a parent's policy usually lets the child, at the rider's expiry age:",
 'Convert to their own policy', ["Receive the rider's face amount", "Become the policy's new owner", 'Name their own beneficiary on it'],
 'Child term riders typically allow each covered child to convert to an individual policy, often a multiple of the rider amount, without evidence of insurability.'),
('Which non-forfeiture option keeps the full face amount in force for a limited time after premiums stop?',
 'Extended term insurance', ['Reduced paid-up insurance', 'Cash surrender value option', 'An automatic premium loan'],
 'Extended term uses the cash value as a single premium for term coverage of the full face amount for as long as it will buy. Reduced paid-up keeps lifelong coverage at a lower amount.'),
('An owner takes a policy loan against the cash value and dies before repaying it. The beneficiary receives:',
 'The death benefit less the loan and interest', ['The full death benefit, as the loan is forgiven', 'Only the cash value left in the policy', 'Nothing until the estate repays the loan'],
 'An unpaid policy loan, with its accrued interest, is deducted from the death benefit at claim. The policy itself secures the loan, so the estate is not asked to repay it.'),
('A client asks why whole life costs more than term for the same amount at age 35. The main reason is that whole life:',
 'Covers for life and builds cash value', ['Pays a larger death benefit than term', 'Is underwritten more strictly than term', 'Includes disability coverage as standard'],
 'Level premiums for lifelong coverage are higher than term because they prefund later years, which creates the cash value. The death benefit and underwriting are the same.'),
('A limited-pay whole life policy, such as 20-pay life, means:',
 'Premiums end at 20 years; cover goes on', ['Coverage ends after 20 years', 'The policy pays out after 20 years', 'Premiums rise sharply once 20 years pass'],
 'Limited-pay policies compress premiums into a set period; the policy is then paid up and coverage continues for life.'),
('A client with a health condition is offered a guaranteed issue policy. A common feature is:',
 'A limited benefit in the first 2 years', ['A larger benefit than standard policies', 'No premium owing for the first year', 'Full benefits from the very first day'],
 'Guaranteed issue asks no health questions, so it usually limits the death benefit to a refund of premiums (sometimes plus interest) for non-accidental deaths in the first two years.'),
]
